    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "APIs are attractive targets because they expose business logic and data. A layered approach keeps them safe."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Security Layers"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "bullet-list",
      "data": {
        "items": [
          "Transport: HTTPS everywhere, HSTS, TLS 1.2+",
          "Authentication: JWT, API keys, OAuth2",
          "Authorization: Roles, policies, resource-based checks",
          "Input validation: Model validation, query limits",
          "Rate limiting: Prevent abuse and DoS",
          "Logging and monitoring: Detect anomalies early"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Common API Threats"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "data": {
        "headers": ["Threat", "Description", "Defense"],
        "rows": [
          ["Brute force", "Repeated login attempts", "Rate limiting, account lockout"],
          ["Data scraping", "Excessive API calls", "Rate limiting, pagination caps"],
          ["Injection", "Malicious input in queries", "Validation, parameterized queries"],
          ["Key leakage", "Exposed API keys in client code", "Backend proxies, short-lived keys"]
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "data": {
        "variant": "info",
        "title": "Defense in Depth",
        "text": "No single control is enough. Combine transport security, identity, validation, and monitoring."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "summary-box",
      "data": {
        "title": "Key Takeaways",
        "points": [
          "APIs need multiple security layers",
          "Rate limiting protects against abuse",
          "API keys should never be exposed in frontend code",
          "Logging is essential for incident response"
        ],
